--
-- 0045 settled the gm nasal on the evidence then available: the Nitya Pooja
-- volume romanises it gum. Transcribing the Sandhyavandanam produced better
-- evidence -- that book spells it gm throughout, four times on book page 20
-- alone (idagm, chandaagmsyaapaha, jyoteegmshyaapaha, ogm), so gum is the
-- outlier and gm is what Giri's own Sandhyavandanam uses.
--
-- ROMAN ONLY. The Devanagari is ꣳ either way. So is the Tamil: it cannot write
-- a bare gm without a vowel, so கும் stands and 0045 already got it right.
-- The reading has not changed -- gum and gm are the same sound, differing only
-- in whether the inherent vowel is written.
-- =============================================================================

begin;

